{"ghsa_id":"GHSA-wpww-v874-ph2p","cve_id":"CVE-2026-100647","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/security-advisories/GHSA-wpww-v874-ph2p","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/security/advisories/GHSA-wpww-v874-ph2p","summary":"vLLM DoS via unbounded `cache_salt` length: multi-hundred-MB salt stalls the single EngineCore scheduler thread (CPU exhaustion)","description":"### Summary\r\n\r\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can exhaust the CPU of the single EngineCore scheduler thread and stall every request on the server by sending HTTP requests with an oversized `cache_salt` parameter. The `cache_salt` field — accepted on the OpenAI-compatible chat completions, completions, responses and pooling endpoints and on the Anthropic `/v1/messages` endpoint — is validated only with `min_length=1` and has **no maximum length**. The full salt string is embedded verbatim into block-0's prefix-cache extra keys and hashed with `sha256(pickle.dumps(...))` **on vLLM's single EngineCore scheduler thread** during request construction, so each request costs O(salt bytes) of serialization plus SHA-256. Measured end-to-end on a live server, a 10 MB salt adds ~58 ms of request latency, 100 MB ~599 ms, and 200 MB ~1195 ms (baseline ~9 ms); under 6 concurrent 100 MB-salt attackers, unrelated ordinary requests are stalled to p90 ≈ 1.8 s / max ≈ 2.5 s (idle median 8.5 ms). Since no HTTP request-body limit is applied by default, a modest stream of such requests keeps the shared scheduler thread busy and denies service to every concurrent tenant. No authentication, no feature flag, and no control-plane/network access are required.\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\n**Attacker-controlled input reaches the engine hot path with no size bound.**\r\n\r\n`cache_salt` is a plain `str` field on four+ API surfaces (latest `main` @ `06569a869`):\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# vllm/entrypoints/openai/completion/protocol.py:199-210 (same shape at\r\n#  chat_completion/protocol.py:467, responses/protocol.py:252, anthropic/protocol.py:141)\r\ncache_salt: str | None = Field(\r\n    default=None,\r\n    min_length=1,               # no max_length\r\n    description=(\r\n        \"If specified, the prefix cache will be salted with the provided \"\r\n        \"string to prevent an attacker to guess prompts in multi-user \"\r\n        \"environments. The salt should be random, protected from \"\r\n        \"access by 3rd parties, and long enough to be \"\r\n        \"unpredictable (e.g., 43 characters base64-encoded, corresponding \"\r\n        \"to 256 bit).\"\r\n    ),\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe value flows to `Request` construction in the EngineCore process, where `update_block_hashes()` → `request_block_hasher` (`vllm/v1/core/kv_cache_utils.py:714-771`) computes the request's block hashes — **on the scheduler thread** (`Request.from_engine_core_request`, `vllm/v1/engine/core.py`). For block 0 the entire salt string is placed into the extra keys:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# vllm/v1/core/kv_cache_utils.py:603-606 (generate_block_hash_extra_keys)\r\ncache_salt_keys: list[str] = (\r\n    [request.cache_salt] if (start_token_idx == 0 and request.cache_salt) else []\r\n)\r\n```\r\n\r\nand hashed:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# vllm/v1/core/kv_cache_utils.py:620-648 (hash_block_tokens)\r\nreturn BlockHash(\r\n    hash_function((parent_block_hash, curr_block_token_ids_tuple, extra_keys))\r\n)\r\n# vllm/utils/hashing.py:26-39 (default caching hash function)\r\ninput_bytes = pickle.dumps(input, protocol=pickle.HIGHEST_PROTOCOL)\r\nreturn hashlib.sha256(input_bytes).digest()\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe salt appears only in block-0's key (so the cost is once per request, not per block), but it is **O(salt bytes)** of pickle serialization plus SHA-256 on the single scheduler thread. Prefix caching (and therefore block hashing) is enabled by default (`enable_prefix_caching=True`), so no non-default flag is needed to reach the sink.\r\n\r\n**End-to-end verification (2026-08-29, real server, vllm==0.28.0 default config, RTX PRO 6000):** end-to-end request latency on a live server grows linearly with salt size, and concurrent large-salt requests stall unrelated ordinary requests:\r\n\r\n```\r\nbaseline (no salt):                  med ≈ 9 ms\r\ncache_salt =  10 MB:                 med ≈ 58 ms\r\ncache_salt =  50 MB:                 med ≈ 301 ms\r\ncache_salt = 100 MB:                 med ≈ 599 ms\r\ncache_salt = 200 MB:                 med ≈ 1195 ms\r\nidle victim (no salt) latency:       med ≈ 8.5 ms\r\nunder 3 concurrent 100 MB-salt\r\nattackers, victim (no salt, 10 reqs): med ≈ 22 ms, max ≈ 1465 ms\r\nunder 6 concurrent 100 MB-salt\r\nattackers, victim (no salt, 20 reqs): p50 ≈ 33 ms, p90 ≈ 1784 ms, max ≈ 2471 ms\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo HTTP request-body limit is applied by vLLM by default, so a single replayable request can carry a multi-hundred-MB body. A stream of such requests occupies the scheduler thread; while it is occupied, other requests on the server are not scheduled (queueing, TTFB blowup, client timeouts) — including unrelated tenants that never send a salt.\r\n\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\n- **Vulnerability type:** unauthenticated remote CPU-exhaustion DoS via an unbounded per-request user field fed into a hot serialization/hash path on the engine's single scheduler thread (CWE-400 / CWE-770 / CWE-20).\r\n- **Impact:** full-service unavailability (scheduling stall) for the duration of the attack; requires no login, no feature flag, no control-plane/network access, and no multi-tenant precondition beyond API reachability.\r\n- **This is the same class as the `n` parameter exhaustion** that vLLM explicitly mitigates: the security guide (`docs/usage/security.md`, \"Request Parameter Resource Limits\") states \"A very large value [of n] causes the engine to allocate memory, CPU, and GPU time proportional to n … To mitigate this, vLLM enforces a configurable upper bound on the n parameter via the VLLM_MAX_N_SEQUENCES environment variable\" — and vLLM has published a related advisory for unbounded `n` (GHSA-3mwp-wvh9-7528). `cache_salt` is an equivalent per-request resource-multiplier input with **no bound**; the documented expected value is ~43 chars (256-bit), making a multi-MB salt unambiguously invalid input that should be rejected by validation alone.\r\n- **Who is impacted:** any multi-user deployment that serves the OpenAI-compatible/Anthropic HTTP API — i.e., the standard deployment. The attacker needs only an HTTP client.\r\n","severity":"medium","author":null,"publisher":{"login":"jperezdealgaba","id":124347725,"node_id":"U_kgDOB2llTQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/124347725?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba","html_url":"https://github.com/jperezdealgaba","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"identifiers":[{"value":"GHSA-wpww-v874-ph2p","type":"GHSA"},{"value":"CVE-2026-100647","type":"CVE"}],"state":"published","created_at":null,"updated_at":"2026-09-30T17:17:29Z","published_at":"2026-09-12T08:49:34Z","closed_at":null,"withdrawn_at":null,"submission":null,"vulnerabilities":[{"package":{"ecosystem":"pip","name":"vllm"},"vulnerable_version_range":"<0.29.0","patched_versions":">=0.29.0","vulnerable_functions":[]}],"cvss_severities":{"cvss_v3":{"vector_stri